Suppose their polynomials converge smoothly and their joint exponentials satisfy, for each $m$, bounds $$\label{eq:joint-exponential-bound}
 \|W_D^m e^{\mathrm it\cdot S_D}\xi\|
 \leq C_m(1+|t|)^{k_m}\|W_D^{r_m}\xi\|,$$ uniformly in $D,t$, and converge strongly on smooth inputs.

Assume explicitly that $$\label{eq:additive-response-parity}
                  H_D(-a)=\mathcal{P} H_D(a)\mathcal{P},
                  \qquad \mathcal{P}\boldsymbol\Omega=\boldsymbol\Omega,$$ where $\mathcal{P}$ is the joint spin/seed symmetry.

outputs.exact_statement:
For every $\eta>0$ there are an integer $p\ge1$, deterministic normalized angles $\gamma,\beta\in\mathbb R^p$, and an integer $D_0\ge2$ such that, for every integer $D\ge D_0$ and every finite simple unweighted $(D+1)$-regular graph $G$ of girth greater than $2p+1$, $$\label{eq:high-girth-maxcut}
 q_{p,G}(2\gamma/\sqrt D,\beta)
 \ge \frac12+\frac{P_*-\eta}{\sqrt D}.$$ The word $p,\gamma,\beta$ is independent of $D$, the graph size, and $G$; the displayed usual MaxCut cost angles, unlike the normalized angles $\gamma$, are scaled with the degree.*


*Let $G_{n,d}$ be uniform among simple labelled $d$-regular graphs on $n$ vertices, along admissible $n$ with $nd$ even. For the same word, put $$\Delta_D(G)=\operatorname{OPT}(G)
             -q_{p,G}(2\gamma/\sqrt D,\beta).$$ The word may be chosen so that $$\label{eq:random-regular-maxcut}
 \lim_{D\to\infty}
 \limsup_{\substack{n\to\infty\\ n(D+1)\ \mathrm{even}}}
 \Pr_G\!\left[\sqrt D\,\Delta_D(G_{n,D+1})>\eta\right]=0.$$ Here the inner limit keeps $D,p,\gamma,\beta$ fixed, and $\Pr_G$ is only over the graph: $q_{p,G}$ already averages quantum measurement.

Let $W$ be an ordinary QAOA word, and apply the same word to the $(D+1)$-regular tree with cost $C_D=D^{-1/2}\sum_{\{i,j\}\in E}Z_iZ_j$, with each unordered edge counted once. Its expectation at an edge is defined by restricting to the finite light cone of that edge. Then $$\label{eq:tree-transfer}
 v_W=\lim_{D\to\infty}\frac{\sqrt D}{2}
       \langle W_D\boldsymbol\Omega\rvert Z_iZ_j\lvert W_D\boldsymbol\Omega\rangle.$$ Here $v_W$ is the disorder-averaged thermodynamic SK energy per vertex, with the normalization in the introduction. Both limits exist for every fixed word and every fixed choice of its angles.

Let $f_D$ be centered, root-invariant, finite-radius vectors on the $(D+1)$-regular tree. Root invariance means that the vector itself is fixed by every rooted permutation of complete child subtrees, including their seed coordinates, at every vertex of its finite neighborhood. Suppose their occupation embeddings converge strongly to $v$ at a fixed finite height and their expected top-generation occupations are bounded uniformly in $D$. Place the same rooted vector at the endpoints of an edge, calling the resulting vectors $f_{D,i}$ and $f_{D,j}$. Then $$\label{eq:energy-shift}
 \lim_{D\to\infty}\frac{\sqrt D}{2}
       \langle f_{D,i},f_{D,j}\rangle
       =\mathcal E(v):=\mathop{\mathrm{Re}}\langle v,\mathsf Lv\rangle.$$ For any centered $v,w$, $$\label{eq:energy-continuity}
 |\mathcal E(v)-\mathcal E(w)|
       \leq(\|v\|+\|w\|)\|v-w\|.$$

Let $T_D$ and $T_D^*$ have uniform smooth bounds on one-copy spaces, and suppose their smooth limits exist. If $\langle \boldsymbol\Omega,T_D\boldsymbol\Omega\rangle=0$, then under the occupation embeddings $$\label{eq:centered-sum-limit}
 D^{-1/2}\sum_{j=1}^D T_D^{(j)}
 \longrightarrow a^*(T\boldsymbol\Omega)+a(T^*\boldsymbol\Omega)$$ smoothly. Without centering, $D^{-1}\sum_jT_D^{(j)}\to\langle \boldsymbol\Omega,T\boldsymbol\Omega\rangle\,1$. Both families have uniform smooth bounds.

Fix a preceding finite sequence as in Theorem <a href="#thm:gate-calculus" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:gate-calculus">14</a>. Suppose a temporary Hamiltonian is diagonal in a common collection of site axes, with coefficients given by an exclusion calculation. Require its local Hamiltonian matrices and every aggregate-input derivative to have polynomial bounds as in Definition <a href="#def:exclusion-gate" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="def:exclusion-gate">13</a>. In a cut branch let $H_D(a)$ denote the sum of the Hamiltonian terms affected by scalar boundary input $a$, truncated beyond its finite influence distance and conjugated by the preceding subtree circuit. Assume explicitly that $$\label{eq:additive-response-parity}
                  H_D(-a)=\mathcal{P} H_D(a)\mathcal{P},
                  \qquad \mathcal{P}\boldsymbol\Omega=\boldsymbol\Omega,$$ where $\mathcal{P}$ is the joint spin/seed symmetry. The first two boundary derivatives have smooth limits $H_\alpha=\lim_D\partial_\alpha H_D(0)$ and $H_{\alpha\lambda}=\lim_D\partial_\alpha\partial_\lambda H_D(0)$, and $H_\alpha$ is centered. These limits concern the response derivatives; no limit for the extensive $H_D(0)$ is required. The change in its $D$ child branches when zero boundary input is replaced by $a/\sqrt D$ is $$\sum_{j=1}^D
   \bigl[H_D(a/\sqrt D)-H_D(0)\bigr]^{(j)}.$$ Its smooth limit is $$\label{eq:additive-cut-response}
 \sum_\alpha a_\alpha q(H_\alpha\boldsymbol\Omega)
 +\frac12\sum_{\alpha,\lambda}a_\alpha a_\lambda
                           \langle H_{\alpha\lambda}\rangle_0.$$ The correction is realized as the self-adjoint generator of the limiting time group of the child cut ratios; the display specifies its action on the smooth domain. We use the same realization after commuting substitution and for the correction generators below. The same formula applies recursively to lower cut responses and to their input jets. It is continuous with the smooth bounds of Theorem <a href="#thm:gate-calculus" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:gate-calculus">14</a>, for a fixed finite list of channels. Commuting parameter operators may replace $a$ under the same exclusions.*


*In particular, if a sequence of such correction generators $R_j$ satisfies $\|R_j\xi\|\leq\delta_j\|W^r\xi\|$ with $\delta_j\to0$, then $$\label{eq:small-generator-error}
 \|(e^{\mathrm itR_j}-1)\xi\|
       \leq |t|\delta_j\|W^r\xi\|.$$ If their exponentials and adjoints also have uniform smooth bounds, these errors tend to zero in every fixed lower graph norm and may be inserted in a fixed surrounding sequence.

Let $h_1,\ldots,h_m$ be a finite list of nonzero vectors in $\mathbb R^2$. There are two perpendicular axes $P_1,P_2$ in the $ZY$-plane with the following property. Set $c_{j,a}=h_j\cdot P_a$, using the coordinates $(Z,Y)$. Given any $\theta\in\mathbb R$, there is a finite list $(a_k,\tau_k)$, with $a_k\in\{1,2\}$, such that $$\label{eq:seed-zero-clocks}
 \sum_{k:a_k=a}\tau_k=0\quad(a=1,2),
 \qquad
 \prod_k e^{-\mathrm i\tau_k c_{j,a_k}P_{a_k}}
 =e^{-\mathrm i\theta X}\quad(1\leq j\leq m).$$ The product is in chronological order, with later factors on the left. The assertion is exact, not merely an assertion about closure.

If <a href="#eq:seed-exact-special-norm" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:seed-exact-special-norm">[eq:seed-exact-special-norm]</a> and <a href="#eq:seed-low-ordinary" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:seed-low-ordinary">[eq:seed-low-ordinary]</a> hold uniformly on bounded input families, then $$\label{eq:seed-insertion-O-r}
 \lVert u_r^*Pu_r\boldsymbol\Omega-A_r^*PA_r\boldsymbol\Omega\rVert=O(r)
 \qquad(P=Y,Z).$$ In particular the actual insertion directions have bounded smooth approximants. No approximation to $u_r^*$ on a high packet is needed.

Under the invariant and the visibility conditions, the echo <a href="#eq:seed-echo-list" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:seed-echo-list">[eq:seed-echo-list]</a> preserves the invariant. Up to a scalar of modulus one its low update is $$\label{eq:seed-echo-low}
 A_r\longmapsto W_Z(g_r)A_r,
 \qquad
 g_r=\frac{t}{r}(v'_r-v_r)=-2t w_r+o(1),$$ where $v_r$ is the actual $Z$-insertion before the forward cost and $v'_r$ is the actual insertion immediately after the selective flip. The special-center update can be taken to be exactly $$\label{eq:seed-D-update}
 D_r\longmapsto D_r-t(v_r+v'_r).$$ All ordinary errors are $o(r)$ before and after the large costs.
